adjtimex: include <unistd.h> for syscall()

On a kernel without __NR_clock_adjtime -- which means anything older than
2.6.39 -- adjtimex() falls through to the __NR_adjtimex branch and calls
syscall() without a declaration in scope. With gcc 14 and later, an implicit
function declaration is an error rather than a warning, so the build stops:

    libc/sysdeps/linux/common/adjtimex.c:18:12: error: implicit declaration
        of function 'syscall'; did you mean '_syscall0'?

The branch is simply unexercised upstream: nobody builds uClibc-ng against a
pre-2.6.39 kernel any more. This device runs 2.6.30.9.

Worth reporting upstream; it is a real bug, not a local quirk.

--- a/libc/sysdeps/linux/common/adjtimex.c
+++ b/libc/sysdeps/linux/common/adjtimex.c
@@ -9,6 +9,7 @@
 #include <sys/syscall.h>
 #include <sys/timex.h>
 #include <time.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 
 int adjtimex(struct timex *buf)
 {
